            <ArticleTitle>The Mental Health Acute Assessment Team: a collaborative approach to treating mental health patients in the community.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>262-265</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1039856216689655</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Many models of community-based mental health crisis teams have been reported. We present our experience of an outreach team made up of a paramedic and mental health nurse.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A proof-of-concept was conducted in Western Sydney. The primary outcome was the proportion of patients where the team were able to facilitate the most appropriate care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Nearly 70% of patients were able to be treated outside the Emergency Department, with about two-thirds being transported directly to a mental health facility.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We have demonstrated that our model of care is successful in enabling appropriate physical and mental health care for patients suffering an acute mental health crisis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
